**User Testing

**Movement to Work — Testing During Prototyping and Development

*Testing Approach
- Informal usability testing was carried out with approximately five people while the designs were being developed.
- Participants were shown individual sections and asked to explore the prototype, try interactions and share what felt clear, confusing or difficult to use.
- Testing was kept conversational rather than following a formal script, allowing people to give straightforward feedback as they used the platform.
- Feedback was gathered throughout prototyping and continued during implementation, rather than being left until the website was finished.

*Areas Tested
- Homepage content, navigation and how easily people could find the main sections.
- Information about the programme, including the two-week journey and how to join.
- Interactive elements such as the About Us crossword and skills puzzle.
- Get Job Ready features, including CV feedback, interview practice and questionnaires.
- Desktop and mobile layouts, focusing on readability, spacing, scrolling and ease of tapping controls.

*Feedback and Common Themes
- Keeping instructions short and visible helped make interactive sections easier to understand.
- Smaller screens needed their own layouts rather than scaled-down versions of desktop pages.
- Important actions and navigation needed to be easy to spot, especially on mobile.
- Busy sections benefited from clearer spacing, larger text and a more obvious order of information.
- Testing the interactions themselves was useful alongside reviewing how the screens looked.

*Changes Made During Prototyping
- Mobile layouts were adjusted to stack content vertically and make better use of narrow screens.
- The two-week programme journey was adapted into a vertical mobile timeline rather than relying on desktop-style horizontal scrolling.
- The skills activity was arranged as a visible grid on mobile, with tapping considered as the main alternative to drag-and-drop.
- Navigation for CV Review and other Get Job Ready tools was adapted into a collapsible mobile panel.
- Cards, buttons and supporting text were reviewed for size, spacing and legibility, including the Join Now and FAQ sections.

*Testing During Implementation
- The same approach continues as working screens replace the Figma prototypes.
- Testing now focuses on whether buttons, menus, progress indicators and tools behave as expected on desktop and mobile.
- Changes are made as issues are noticed, then checked again to see whether the experience is clearer.
- Further checks are still needed across different devices, especially for keyboard use, smaller screens and the longer interactive flows.

**Overall Outcome
- User feedback has been used to guide small, ongoing improvements rather than one large redesign at the end.
- The main focus has been making the platform easier to understand and navigate while keeping its visual style and interactive features.
- This is informal feedback from a small group, so it helps identify usability issues but does not prove that every user will have the same experience.
